BATCH={'key':'ia_dubuque_jhgj','label':'Dubuque, Iowa','added':'2026-11-24 02:31'}
PLACES=[
N('julien-dubuque-monument','Julien Dubuque Monument','Dubuque','ia','curio','standing',42.46886,-90.64634,'exact','public','walk','','1897–',
  'A round stone tower on the bluff above the Mississippi at the south edge of Dubuque, put up in 1897 by the city\'s leaders to honour Julien Dubuque, the French-Canadian lead miner they counted as the town\'s founder. Human remains were found when its footings were dug, but they were never identified, so it is not certain that Dubuque himself lies here. It stands in the Mines of Spain State Recreation Area, among the archaeological sites of his mining and smelting venture, which are a National Historic Landmark district.',
  'A cylindrical tower about 25 feet high and 12 feet across, of locally quarried limestone with walls 18 inches thick and no roof. It has narrow windows, a grille over the doorway and a bronze plaque with Julien Dubuque\'s name. A road, paths, benches and signs were added round it in the 20th century; the view down the river is the other reason to come.',
  'A roofless castle turret for a man who may or may not be buried under it.',
  'Iowa Department of Natural Resources, Mines of Spain State Recreation Area. Parking at the end of Monument Drive, about 160 m west of the tower. Iowa state parks are open 4 a.m. to 10:30 p.m. (the DNR\'s rule, as web search results quoted it; the DNR page itself was not read). Park office, 8991 Bellevue Heights.',
  [W('Mines of Spain State Recreation Area and E. B. Lyons Nature Center')],
  th=[42.46863,-90.64827], thn='Monument Drive parking lot',
  brick='no', hours='Park open daily 4 a.m. to 10:30 p.m.'),
N('town-clock-dubuque','Town Clock','Dubuque','ia','curio','standing',42.49996,-90.66670,'exact','public','walk','','1873–',
  'Dubuque\'s first town clock stood on the John Bell store on Main Street; on May 25, 1872 the building gave way under it and clock and store collapsed together, killing a child and two women. Its replacement, built by Howard & Co. of Boston with a bell of over 4,200 pounds cast by Naylor & Co., started on April 17, 1873 on a tower over a new three-storey Italianate building at 823 Main Street. When Main Street was made a pedestrian plaza in the urban renewal years, the tower was taken down and bolted onto a new four-column pedestal in Town Clock Plaza in February 1971, about 108 feet above the street. When Main Street was reopened to cars a small traffic circle was built round it.',
  'A tall clock tower and cupola standing on a four-column concrete pedestal of 1971 in the middle of Main Street, ringed by a traffic circle. The 1873 building it first stood on, at 823 to 825 Main Street, still stands a block away and is on the National Register as the Town Clock Building.',
  'A clock tower lifted off its building and set down in the middle of the street.',
  'City street, Main Street between Eighth and Ninth streets, downtown. Seen from the sidewalks; street parking.',
  [W('Town Clock (Dubuque, Iowa)')],
  brick='?'),
N('carnegie-stout-public-library','Carnegie-Stout Public Library','Dubuque','ia','civic','standing',42.50256,-90.66987,'exact','open','walk','','1901–1902',
  'Andrew Carnegie gave $71,500 in January 1901 for a library building, and the local businessman Frank D. Stout gave the land in his father\'s honour. The Chicago architects W. G. Williamson and John Spencer designed it, and it opened in October 1902; it is counted among the best Beaux-Arts buildings in Iowa. The collection began with the Young Men\'s Literary Association of 1859. An addition went on the east side in 1981.',
  'A two-storey building of Bedford stone and brick whose front is a Roman Corinthian portico modelled on the Pantheon, with fluted columns and matching pilasters behind them. It is a working public library, so anyone may go in during opening hours; what survives of the original interior was not checked.',
  'Carnegie\'s money and a Pantheon portico on a Dubuque side street.',
  'City public library, 360 West 11th Street, on Jackson Park. Hours from the library\'s own page.',
  [W('Carnegie-Stout Public Library'),L('Carnegie-Stout Public Library','https://carnegiestout.org/')],
  brick='?', hours='Monday to Thursday 9 a.m. to 7 p.m.; Friday and Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Sunday noon to 5 p.m.'),
N('st-lukes-methodist-dubuque','St. Luke\'s United Methodist Church','Dubuque','ia','worship','standing',42.50381,-90.66898,'exact','view','walk','','1896–1897',
  'The congregation traces itself to the first sermon preached on Iowa soil, in November 1833, and met first in a log building on what is now Washington Square. After churches of 1834, 1839 and 1853, it built this Richardsonian Romanesque church at 12th and Main streets in 1896 and moved in in 1897. It is the only church of that style in Dubuque and is on the National Register for its architecture and its glass.',
  'More than 100 windows by Tiffany, more than any other church in Iowa, restored in stages since 1990; one shows an angel among lilies. The organ is a Farrand & Votey of 36 ranks and 2,060 pipes, rebuilt in 1992. Visiting times and tours could not be read on the church\'s own page; interior visits not checked.',
  'A Dubuque church with more Tiffany windows than any other in Iowa.',
  'Active United Methodist congregation, 1199 Main Street at 12th Street. Seen from the street; the church\'s own page could not be opened, so service times and tour hours were not checked. Street parking.',
  [W('St. Luke\'s United Methodist Church (Dubuque, Iowa)')],
  brick='?'),
N('dubuque-brewing-and-malting','Dubuque Brewing and Malting Company','Dubuque','ia','industry','ruins',42.52701,-90.67954,'site','view','drive','','1894–1896',
  'Four of Dubuque\'s big breweries merged in 1892 to form the Dubuque Brewing and Malting Company, which built this plant at 30th and Jackson streets: construction began in 1895 and the brewery opened on May 7, 1896. Louis Lehle of Chicago, a well-known designer of breweries, drew it, with the Dubuque firm of Fridolin Heer and Son supervising. After years empty, a renovation into apartments fell through; part of the roof collapsed in 2016, the city closed the streets round it as dangerous in 2023, and from April 30, 2024 the southern part with two of its towers was knocked down. The northern part was left standing for possible reuse, and the city reported the demolition complete in November 2024.',
  'What stands is the northern part of a Richardsonian Romanesque pile that once covered some three acres: stone first floor, brick above, with towers under steep roofs and corner pinnacles. The rest is a cleared or rubble site. Exactly how much was left after November 2024 was not checked on the ground.',
  'A brewery built like a castle, now half a castle.',
  'Privately owned and offered for sale; closed and unsafe. Look from Jackson Street or East 30th Street only, and keep clear of the walls. Street parking.',
  [L('Encyclopedia Dubuque: Dubuque Brewing and Malting Company','https://encyclopediadubuque.org/index.php/DUBUQUE_BREWING_AND_MALTING_COMPANY'),L('SAH Archipedia','https://sah-archipedia.org/buildings/IA-01-ME188')],
  conf='mixed', brick='?'),
N('dubuque-city-hall','Dubuque City Hall','Dubuque','ia','civic','standing',42.50522,-90.66769,'exact','open','walk','','1857–1858',
  'Designed by J. N. Moody after Faneuil Hall in Boston and the Fulton Street Market in New York, with the Dubuque architect John F. Rague supervising, and first used in February 1858. In the old way, the city market took the ground floor, the city offices the second and a ballroom the third; the market stalls later became offices and the ballroom a police pistol range and bowling alley. The decayed cupola with its bell and clock was taken down in 1954 and a copy put up in 1990, with the bell back in it. It is still City Hall.',
  'Three storeys of brick on a raised limestone basement, with a gable roof on brackets, a round window in each gable and tall arched window bays 11 feet high, under the 1990 cupola. Office interior, much changed; original fittings not checked.',
  'A city hall built over a market, after Boston\'s Faneuil Hall.',
  'City of Dubuque offices, 50 West 13th Street. A public building; office hours were not found on the city\'s page. Street parking.',
  [W('Dubuque City Hall'),L('City of Dubuque','https://www.cityofdubuque.org/')],
  brick='?', hours='?'),
N('dubuque-county-courthouse','Dubuque County Courthouse','Dubuque','ia','civic','standing',42.50096,-90.66447,'exact','open','walk','','1891–1893',
  'Built from 1891 to 1893 on the site of the county\'s first, red-brick courthouse of the 1840s, to designs by the Dubuque architect Fridolin Heer. It is Beaux-Arts with Renaissance and Romanesque touches, five storeys under a central tower topped by a 14-foot statue of Justice, 213 feet to the top. It still holds the county offices and the district court.',
  'Bedford limestone on the ground floor and red brick with moulded terra cotta above. Twelve pewter statues once stood on the roof; six remain. Inside, the tower shaft ends on the fourth floor in a stained-glass dome, and the oak woodwork is carved in a Richardsonian manner. A glass elevator and a granite fountain came in the 1980s. Visitors go through screening at the one open entrance.',
  'A 213-foot courthouse with a gilded dome and half its rooftop statues.',
  'Dubuque County, Central Avenue between 7th and 8th streets. A public building open in office hours; the county\'s page could not be opened, so the hours were not checked. Street parking.',
  [W('Dubuque County Courthouse')],
  brick='yes', hours='?'),
N('dubuque-county-jail','Dubuque County Jail','Dubuque','ia','civic','standing',42.50130,-90.66458,'exact','view','walk','','1856–1858',
  'Built in 1856 to 1858 of blue limestone from Dunleith, Illinois, to a design by John F. Rague (of the Illinois and Iowa territorial capitols), with thirty-two cells and a sheriff\'s house; a six-cell wing for women followed in 1874. It is one of the few Egyptian Revival buildings in the country and a National Historic Landmark. It was a jail until 1971, an art gallery from 1975, then the Old Jail Museum, and in 2016 it became county offices, restored at a cost of $800,000.',
  'A low limestone block in the Egyptian Revival style beside the courthouse, at 36 East 8th Street. Now county offices, so seen from the street; the inside was not checked.',
  'An Egyptian temple of a jail, a National Historic Landmark.',
  'Dubuque County offices, 36 East 8th Street, beside the courthouse. Seen from the street. Street parking.',
  [W('Dubuque County Jail')],
  brick='no'),
N('five-flags-theater-dubuque','Five Flags Theater (Majestic Theater)','Dubuque','ia','civic','standing',42.49787,-90.66617,'exact','open','walk','','1910–',
  'A theatre stood on this corner from 1840. The present one was built as the Majestic Theater in 1910, after its predecessor burned, to designs by George L. Rapp, who became one of the country\'s leading theatre architects. It turned to films in 1920, was renamed the Spensley in 1929 and later the RKO Orpheum, and was set for demolition in 1969; a citizens\' fund drive saved it, and it reopened in 1976 as the Five Flags Theater, part of the Five Flags Center with an arena built behind it in 1979. It is on the National Register as the Orpheum Theatre and Site.',
  'A Renaissance Revival front with French touches and a mansard roof. It is the home of the Dubuque Symphony Orchestra, so the hall is seen by going to a performance; the interior was not checked.',
  'A Rapp theatre saved from urban renewal by a fund drive.',
  'City-owned, run as part of the Five Flags Center, 405 Main Street. Open for shows; box office times were not found on its page. Street and ramp parking downtown.',
  [W('Five Flags Center'),L('Five Flags Center','https://www.fiveflagscenter.com/')],
  brick='?', hours='?'),
N('st-raphaels-cathedral-dubuque','St. Raphael\'s Cathedral','Dubuque','ia','worship','standing',42.49504,-90.66732,'exact','view','walk','','1857–1861',
  'The parish, founded by Father Samuel Mazzuchelli in the 1830s, is the oldest congregation of any Christian church in Iowa, and its first stone church was the first Catholic church in the state. Bishop Mathias Loras laid the cornerstone of this Gothic Revival cathedral, designed by the Dubuque architect John Mullany, on July 5, 1857; it was finished in 1861 and its tower in November 1876. A ten-million-dollar renovation of the church and its grounds was finished in April 2025.',
  'A brick church on a raised basement and stone foundation, 160 by 83 feet, with a 130-foot central tower of limestone below and, above, a bell chamber of wood sheathed in galvanized iron painted to look like stone (the planned 243-foot spire was never built after the front wall cracked). Stained glass from London in the nave, from 1889; the Blessed Sacrament Chapel of 1882 behind; and the relics of the Roman martyr Cessianus in the main altar. Visiting outside Mass not checked.',
  'A tower that is stone at the bottom and painted tin at the top.',
  'Active Catholic cathedral of the Archdiocese of Dubuque, 231 Bluff Street, in the Cathedral Historic District. The parish\'s own page could not be opened, so Mass times were not checked. Parking lot at the church; street parking.',
  [W('St. Raphael\'s Cathedral (Dubuque, Iowa)')],
  brick='?'),
N('hotel-julien-dubuque','Hotel Julien Dubuque','Dubuque','ia','hotel','standing',42.49635,-90.66431,'exact','open','walk','','1913–1915',
  'A hotel has stood at Second and Main streets since 1839, first the Waples House and from 1854 the Julien House, where Abraham Lincoln is believed to have stayed. Fire destroyed the Julien House in 1913, and the present building was put up from soon after and finished in 1915. Local lore has Al Capone using it as a hideout. After a restoration of its outside and its lobby and ballroom, begun in 2007, it reopened in 2009 under its present name.',
  'A many-storeyed downtown hotel of 1915 with its lobby and grand ballroom restored and a mirrored staircase from the lobby to the second floor. Guests and diners go in; other visitors not checked.',
  'The hotel that rose from the 1913 fire, and Capone\'s Dubuque bolt-hole by local story.',
  'Working hotel, 200 Main Street, at Second Street. Open to guests and to diners at its restaurant and lounge; the hotel\'s page gave no hours for visitors. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Hotel Julien Dubuque'),L('Hotel Julien Dubuque','https://www.hoteljuliendubuque.com/')],
  brick='?', hours='?'),
N('new-melleray-abbey','New Melleray Abbey','Peosta','ia','worship','standing',42.39001,-90.80902,'site','open','drive','','1849–',
  'A Trappist monastery founded on July 16, 1849 by monks from Mount Melleray Abbey in Ireland, invited by Bishop Mathias Loras; six of the second party died of cholera on the way up the Mississippi. After the Civil War the monks built the present stone monastery in a 13th-century Gothic style to plans by the Dubuque architect John Mullany. The church was finished only in the 1970s, when the whole north wing was made into it; its design won an American Institute of Architects honour award in 1977. The monks support themselves by making wooden caskets.',
  'A long Gothic stone monastery about 15 miles southwest of Dubuque. The church in the north wing has red oak choir stalls and doors and a five-ton granite altar. Visitors may come to pray or to join the monks\' services, and there is a guest house for retreats.',
  'Monks who wake at 3:30 a.m. and build coffins.',
  'Cistercian monastery, 6632 Melleray Circle, Peosta. Parking at the abbey. The church\'s visiting hours are from the abbey\'s hospitality page as a web search result quoted it; the page itself would not open. Be quiet in the church; the cloister is the monks\' home.',
  [W('New Melleray Abbey'),L('New Melleray Abbey','https://newmelleray.org/')],
  th=[42.39021,-90.80940], thn='New Melleray Abbey parking',
  brick='no', hours='Church open daily 4:30 a.m. to 8 p.m.'),
N('wartburg-seminary-dubuque','Wartburg Theological Seminary','Dubuque','ia','inst','standing',42.48528,-90.69143,'site','view','walk','','1914–1916',
  'A Lutheran seminary (now of the Evangelical Lutheran Church in America) that came out of Wilhelm Loehe\'s mission colonies, named after the castle where Martin Luther hid in 1521. It moved back to Dubuque in 1889. Plans for new buildings were drawn in 1914, and the buildings, of Galena limestone quarried nearby along Fremont Avenue and made to look like the Wartburg in Eisenach, Germany, were dedicated in September 1916 with services in German.',
  'A castle-like group of limestone buildings on the bluff at 333 Wartburg Place. A working school: the grounds and inside were not checked.',
  'A German castle copied in Iowa limestone for a seminary.',
  'Wartburg Theological Seminary, 333 Wartburg Place. A private school; seen from the street. Its main office is open Monday to Friday, 8 a.m. to 4 p.m. (from its page). Parking not checked.',
  [W('Wartburg Theological Seminary'),L('Encyclopedia Dubuque: Wartburg Theological Seminary','https://encyclopediadubuque.org/index.php/WARTBURG_THEOLOGICAL_SEMINARY')],
  brick='no'),
N('st-marys-church-dubuque','St. Mary\'s Church (Steeple Square)','Dubuque','ia','worship','standing',42.50780,-90.66716,'site','view','walk','','1864–1867',
  'Dubuque\'s German Catholics, crowded out of the cathedral, formed Holy Trinity parish in 1849 and built this much larger church, designed by John Mullany, from 1864 to 1867; its steeple is one of the tallest in the area. The parish closed with a final Mass in May 2010. The church, school and other parish buildings are now Steeple Square, a nonprofit with an event centre, a community kitchen, childcare and housing in the old school; the steeple was rebuilt with a 2016 grant.',
  'A Gothic Revival church at 15th and White streets with its tall spire. Inside, a Hook and Hastings organ in its case of the early 1870s, twelve large windows to the Virgin Mary made in Bavaria (installed in 1914 by one account, 1916 by another, and restored in 2017) and a mural of the Assumption behind the altar. It is used for events; when the public may look in was not checked.',
  'A closed parish church saved as Steeple Square.',
  'Steeple Square, a nonprofit, 101 East 15th Street. Seen from the street; open for its events. Street parking.',
  [W('Saint Mary\'s Catholic Church (Dubuque, Iowa)'),L('Encyclopedia Dubuque: Steeple Square','https://encyclopediadubuque.org/index.php/STEEPLE_SQUARE')],
  conf='mixed', brick='?'),
]
